# and the write-up's disclosure claim was judged against that.
version: v1.2.0
date-released: "2026-08-05"
abstract: >-
  Measurement harnesses used to audit how Python conformal-prediction
  implementations resolve the conformal quantile at finite sample sizes. Each
  script re-derives its own formulas with `fractions.Fraction` the moment it
  loads, and each writes a plain-text output file that is committed alongside it.
  Covers the convention in isolation; the nine Hyndman-Fan quantile definitions
  read as carriers of a coverage guarantee; end-to-end runs through sktime,
  river, darts and statsforecast; paired real-data coverage on two Monash
  collections and two OpenML suites, in which the library arm and a
  required-rank arm are built from the same scores and the same point
  prediction; a census of the level-to-rank resolution sites across the audited
  packages, verified on disk; a classifier that takes an arbitrary quantile
  helper and reports what it delivers; a check of whether the mechanism
  generalises beyond conformal prediction to value-at-risk, tolerance bounds and
  bootstrap intervals; and a rebuttal probe that adjudicates a retracted finding
  of the author's own.
keywords:
  - conformal prediction
  - uncertainty quantification
  - finite-sample coverage
  - order statistics
  - prediction intervals
  - software correctness
  - reproducibility
